# Ask the BOOT partition how it is doing. The check cannot write: fsck.vfat -n
# opens the volume read-only and only reports, so this is safe to run any time,
# mounted or not, window open or not. It exits 0 on a volume that needs no
# repair, 1 on one that does, 3 when the FAT could not be read at all, and 4
# when the volume has something to say that this machine put there: while a
# boot-rw window is open the kernel holds the FAT dirty bit set as its own
# record of an unclean unmount, so the card itself cannot be judged until the
# window is closed.

exec @SBINDIR@/bootmount.sh check
